{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: <strong>pandas</strong>\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Why **pandas**?\n",
        "\n",
        "# What is **pandas**?\n",
        "\n",
        "- In its own words:\n",
        "\n",
        "> a fast, powerful, flexible and easy to use open source data analysis and manipulation tool\n",
        "\n",
        "- In my words:\n",
        "\n",
        "\"The basis of the modern 'boom' in data and data analysis\"\n",
        "\n",
        "## **pandas**\n",
        "\n",
        "- Stands for \"panel data\"\n",
        "- Original built on NumPy, now on supercomputing technologies.\n",
        "- Core innovation: The DataFrame\n",
        "\n",
        "> [A DataFrame is a data structure that organizes data into a 2-dimensional table of rows and columns, much like a spreadsheet.](https://www.databricks.com/glossary/what-are-dataframes)\n",
        "\n",
        "## \"like a spreadsheet\"\n",
        "\n",
        "- With **pandas**, we can do all the things we can do in a spreadsheet, but:\n",
        "  \t- Automatically\n",
        "\t- Repeatedly\n",
        "\t- Consistently\n",
        "- Basically, we never have to find a button again, but instead we have to learn functions from code documentation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Spreadsheet Usage\n",
        "\n",
        "- I mostly (used to) use spreadsheets to make charts.\n",
        "- **pandas** works *very* well with Matplotlib (better than NumPy does, I think)\n",
        "- In the NumPy tutorial, **pandas** and Matplotlib are the two other Python packages mentioned, to read spreadsheets and plot them, respectively.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why not **pandas**?\n",
        "\n",
        "- After years undisputed prominence as the best data library, **pandas** has recently received a challenger called \"Polars\" which is situationally faster and rapidly gaining popularity.\n",
        "- I expect Polars to take over in cloud computing but not in scientific computing.\n",
        "- Polars uses neither NumPy not Matplotlib, but plots with Altair, which I found insuitable for scientific applications.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Relevance\n",
        "\n",
        "- We have only calculated and plotted data we have typed in ourselves!\n",
        "- Yuck!\n",
        "\n",
        "# Install\n",
        "\n",
        "## pip again\n",
        "\n",
        "- Just like NumPy and Matplotlib, **pandas** is a Python package which we install via `pip`\n",
        "```{.bash code-line-numbers=\"false\"}\n",
        "python3 -m pip install pandas\n",
        "```\n",
        "- That might take a moment, when it does we can check it worked!\n",
        "\n",
        "## Two First Steps\n",
        "\n",
        "- There are two *great* ways to get a **pandas** DataFrame.\n",
        "- We quickly show both."
      ],
      "id": "3c46050c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd"
      ],
      "id": "3ab15645",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## From NumPy\n"
      ],
      "id": "29e21ba7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "taxes = np.array([\n",
        "    [9275, .1],\n",
        "    [37650, .15],\n",
        "    [91150, .25],\n",
        "    [190150, .28],\n",
        "    [413350, .33],\n",
        "    [415051, .35]\n",
        "])\n",
        "df = pd.DataFrame(taxes) # use \"df\" for dataframes by convention\n",
        "df # You'll notice this may look a lot nicer"
      ],
      "id": "75e226c7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Get a File\n",
        "\n",
        "- I will use some nuclear data.\n",
        "- Usually data will come from your research group or experiments.\n",
        "- Often stored as a CSV.\n",
        "  \t- A \".csv\" file, for \"comma separated value\"\n",
        "- We'll use \"Periodic Table of Elements.csv\"\n",
        "  \t- Spaces in maes are annoying; we'll manage.\n",
        "- It's from [here](https://gist.github.com/GoodmanSciences/c2dd862cd38f21b0ad36b8f96b4bf1ee)\n",
        "\n",
        "## From a URL\n",
        "\n",
        "- You can use files on your computer, or...\n",
        "- From a url.\n",
        "- But the url must be the address *of the file*\n",
        "  - Not a page that talks about the file.\n",
        "  - Not a page with the same data but presented in a pretty table.\n",
        "\n",
        "## \"Raw\" Files\n",
        "\n",
        "- On GitHub (common place to keep files) these are called \"raw\" files.\n",
        "  - [https://gist.githubusercontent.com/GoodmanSciences/c2dd862cd38f21b0ad36b8f96b4bf1ee/raw/1d92663004489a5b6926e944c1b3d9ec5c40900e/Periodic%2520Table%2520of%2520Elements.csv](https://gist.githubusercontent.com/GoodmanSciences/c2dd862cd38f21b0ad36b8f96b4bf1ee/raw/1d92663004489a5b6926e944c1b3d9ec5c40900e/Periodic%2520Table%2520of%2520Elements.csv)\n",
        "- Note - that ends in `.csv`\n",
        "\n",
        "## From File\n"
      ],
      "id": "4f73de61"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df = pd.read_csv(\"https://gist.githubusercontent.com/GoodmanSciences/c2dd862cd38f21b0ad36b8f96b4bf1ee/raw/1d92663004489a5b6926e944c1b3d9ec5c40900e/Periodic%2520Table%2520of%2520Elements.csv\")\n",
        "df"
      ],
      "id": "9d52af28",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# DataFrames\n",
        "\n",
        "## DataFrames and Arrays\n",
        "\n",
        "- DataFrames:\n",
        "  \t- Have row and column labels\n",
        "\t- Can store multiple types of data (strings/words, integers, and floating point numbers)\n",
        "- Arrays:\n",
        "  \t- Don't\n",
        "\t- Can't\n",
        "\n",
        "## Subsetting\n",
        "\n",
        "- One of the most common uses for **pandas** is to *subset*, to look at part of DataFrame but not all.\n",
        "- Usually by looking at a specific column or columns - by name.\n",
        "  \t- Vs. arrays and lists, DataFrames still have indices, but they are now usually strings."
      ],
      "id": "8da79542"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[\"Element\"]"
      ],
      "id": "76d39c3b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Multiple Indices\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can also subset multiple indices using a *list* of *indices* (column names).\n"
      ],
      "id": "fc75f1d3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[[\"Element\",\"Symbol\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "34663cd9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Filtering\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can subset rows by *filtering*\n",
        "- Basically we write what would be an `if` statement, but use it as an index.\n",
        "- The \"transuranic\" elements are elements higher than number 92."
      ],
      "id": "89e1b230"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[df[\"AtomicNumber\"] > 92]"
      ],
      "id": "7cbdda18",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Double `df`\n",
        "\n",
        "- We use `df` twice in that expression:\n",
        "  - Once to check the `df` if some value is greater than 92\n",
        "  - Once to look up all elements *for which* the value is greater."
      ],
      "id": "238552c7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[\"AtomicNumber\"] > 92"
      ],
      "id": "aad060e6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## .columns\n",
        "\n",
        "- By the way, what all do we know?"
      ],
      "id": "61ba436e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.columns"
      ],
      "id": "30dee8ac",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- Oh that's a lot of information!\n",
        "\n",
        "## How big?\n",
        "\n",
        "- How big is `df`?"
      ],
      "id": "1ea056e8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.shape"
      ],
      "id": "f8f357ae",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- And by the way, `np.array`'s have `shape` too."
      ],
      "id": "aaba6a08"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "taxes.shape"
      ],
      "id": "82b31f9f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- Aside: Multiple values within a `()` are asically lists but called tuples."
      ],
      "id": "48f05fe7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "type(taxes.shape)"
      ],
      "id": "a58b9c43",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Locating Entries\n",
        "\n",
        "- Sometimes you want to use an integer index. "
      ],
      "id": "0e70d7cf"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.iloc[10] # \"Integer Location\""
      ],
      "id": "4c5a65b3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Zero/One index\n",
        "\n",
        "- DataFrames are 0-indexed"
      ],
      "id": "ef02aa67"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[\"AtomicNumber\"].iloc[0]"
      ],
      "id": "f0ea8545",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- Elements are 1-indexed[^1] with good reason."
      ],
      "id": "c7d491df"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[[\"AtomicNumber\",\"NumberofProtons\"]].head(1)"
      ],
      "id": "d4af6f87",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "[^1]: Neutronium is a proposed but not academically interesting element with atomic number `0`.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data Manipulation\n",
        "\n",
        "- Check out these columns"
      ],
      "id": "a9a99123"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.iloc[::20][[\"NumberofNeutrons\", \"NumberofProtons\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "79feb57e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Pattern\n",
        "\n",
        "- It *looks* like atoms with fewer protons have the same number of protons and neutrons, and atoms with many protons have many more neutrons.\n",
        "- Let's calculate the difference."
      ],
      "id": "c4824806"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[\"NumberofProtons\"] - df[\"NumberofNeutrons\"]"
      ],
      "id": "856d4034",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Plots\n",
        "\n",
        "## Visualize\n",
        "\n",
        "- Easier to visualize."
      ],
      "id": "808263eb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# import Matplotlib if you don't have it yet!\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
      ],
      "id": "9c2bf389",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Scatterplot\n"
      ],
      "id": "a42ad262"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.scatter(df[\"NumberofProtons\"], df[\"NumberofNeutrons\"])"
      ],
      "id": "675f37e2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Insights\n",
        "\n",
        "- A lot of science, as far as I can tell is finding novel insights.\n",
        "- I noticed that boiling/melting point seemed to go up and down at some intervals.\n",
        "- I recall that the number of outermost electrons did that too...\n",
        "- Let's make `num_es` again, from last time.\n",
        "\n",
        "## `num_es`\n"
      ],
      "id": "7ccc116f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "es = np.array([2, 8, 8, 18, 18, 32, 32])\n",
        "num_es = np.array([]) # The first zero elements\n",
        "for e in es:\n",
        "    num_es = np.append(num_es, np.arange(e))\n",
        "num_es += 1\n",
        "num_es"
      ],
      "id": "04a07c18",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Expanding DataFrames\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can create a new column and add new data to it from a `np.array`\n",
        "- At least if the dimensions work out..."
      ],
      "id": "76398ecc"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.shape, num_es.shape"
      ],
      "id": "b788d881",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- We have 118 elements, so it is good we have 118 possible outermost electron counts!\n",
        "\n",
        "## Update list\n",
        "\n",
        "- Like updating the element of a list by index..."
      ],
      "id": "f302de22"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "colors = ['red', 'orange', 'yellow', 'green', 'blue', 'indigo', 'violet']\n",
        "colors[-1] = \"purple\"\n",
        "colors"
      ],
      "id": "7c36ff1b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Update DataFrame\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can update *or add* a column to a DataFrame by index:"
      ],
      "id": "e49d120a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[\"OutermostElectrons\"] = num_es\n",
        "df.iloc[::20][[\"Element\",\"OutermostElectrons\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "633d1232",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Patterns\n",
        "\n",
        "- For a scatter, that is two (`2`) DataFrames (or subsets), **not** one DataFrame with two columns\n"
      ],
      "id": "9e1b3a6c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.scatter(df[\"OutermostElectrons\"], df[\"MeltingPoint\"])"
      ],
      "id": "959b220f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 3D\n",
        "\n",
        "- Third dimension via `s=` for size \n"
      ],
      "id": "76b1404b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.scatter(df[\"OutermostElectrons\"], df[\"MeltingPoint\"], s=df[\"AtomicNumber\"])"
      ],
      "id": "0643dd44",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Columns to Columns\n",
        "\n",
        "- Using vector operations, we can make novel columns from existing columns."
      ],
      "id": "7dae6a95"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[\"NeutronsLessProtons\"] = df[\"NumberofNeutrons\"] - df[\"NumberofProtons\"]\n",
        "plt.plot(df[\"NeutronsLessProtons\"])"
      ],
      "id": "39b98ed4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 4D\n"
      ],
      "id": "bc3a5e5a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.scatter(x=df[\"OutermostElectrons\"], # we can optional specify x and y \n",
        "            y=df[\"MeltingPoint\"], \n",
        "            s=df[\"AtomicNumber\"], \n",
        "            c=df[\"NeutronsLessProtons\"]) # color\n",
        "plt.colorbar() # Like a legend for colors."
      ],
      "id": "b9707854",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \"kwargs\"\n",
        "\n",
        "- When we call a function and use something like `x=` in the arguments, `x` is a keyword so we call these \"kwargs\" for \"keyword arguments\".\n",
        "- You may see this when looking up functions.\n",
        "- Just follow examples."
      ],
      "id": "cc224b97"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def triple(x):\n",
        "\treturn 3 * x\n",
        "\n",
        "[triple(x=7), triple(7)]"
      ],
      "id": "3ebf5e24",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Dropping\n",
        "\n",
        "- Some columns may be useless or redundant."
      ],
      "id": "c681ed3a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.iloc[::20][[\"AtomicNumber\",\"NumberofProtons\",\"NumberofElectrons\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "857bd0ad",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Drop it\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can remove such columns."
      ],
      "id": "5e1fd192"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.shape"
      ],
      "id": "1c365158",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Have to specify columns (we can also drop rows)\n",
        "df = df.drop(columns=[\"NumberofProtons\",\"NumberofElectrons\"])\n",
        "df.shape"
      ],
      "id": "02e4ebde",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Summaries\n",
        "\n",
        "## Summary Statistics\n",
        "\n",
        "- There's a variety of ways to describe a DataFrame other than just seeing all of it's data.\n"
      ],
      "id": "2697d9b7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.describe()"
      ],
      "id": "72314ce7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Info\n",
        "\n",
        "- Info is often helpful\n"
      ],
      "id": "306dd390"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.info()"
      ],
      "id": "9fc743bc",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Columns\n",
        "\n",
        "- And remember we can see all the column names!\n",
        "- No `()`!\n"
      ],
      "id": "64a8ff75"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.columns"
      ],
      "id": "7fb32a16",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Shape\n",
        "\n",
        "- And of course shape!\n"
      ],
      "id": "5abf1fac"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.shape"
      ],
      "id": "eaa1f53e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Column-wise\n",
        "\n",
        "- We can do all kinds of operations over a single column."
      ],
      "id": "c5963ddb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "mp = df[\"MeltingPoint\"] # Copy column to save some typing.\n",
        "[mp.min(), mp.max(), mp.mean(), mp.median(), mp.mode(), mp.std()]"
      ],
      "id": "8f6f3778",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Using `loc`\n",
        "\n",
        "## Metal or not\n",
        "\n",
        "- We helpfully can already see what metals are."
      ],
      "id": "22320cc7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.iloc[15:25:2][[\"Element\",\"Metal\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "a96ca55e",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## NaN\n",
        "\n",
        "- `NaN` means \"not a number\" and is used by **pandas** (not generally by Python) to fill in missing data."
      ],
      "id": "c63d1dac"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.sqrt(-1)"
      ],
      "id": "8790f4ed",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- I find this idea a bit odd (and e.g. Polars does not do this) but we can work with it.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## (Non)metal(loid)\n",
        "\n",
        "- Elements are \"metal\", \"nonmetal\", or \"metalloid\".\n",
        "- I regard this as how *metallic* an element is."
      ],
      "id": "fdc8cfa5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.iloc[12:16][[\"Element\",\"Metal\",\"Nonmetal\",\"Metalloid\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "f995c0f9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Metallic \n",
        "\n",
        "- We introduce and use `np.where`, which takes\n",
        "  - A condition\n",
        "  - A value if condition\n",
        "  - A value else"
      ],
      "id": "7cbde3c6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[\"Metallic\"] = np.where(df[\"Metal\"] == \"yes\", \"Metal\", \"Nonmetal\")\n",
        "df.iloc[12:16][[\"Metal\",\"Nonmetal\",\"Metalloid\",\"Metallic\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "bf0ef197",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Metalloids\n",
        "\n",
        "- Additionally deal with metalloids."
      ],
      "id": "bd9ac215"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[df[\"Metalloid\"] == \"yes\"][\"Metallic\"] = \"Metalloid\"\n",
        "df.iloc[12:16][[\"Metal\",\"Nonmetal\",\"Metalloid\",\"Metallic\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "d168f9fe",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- Whoops!\n",
        "\n",
        "## `loc`\n",
        "\n",
        "- `df[df[\"Metalloid\"] == \"yes\"]` is not the same dataframe as `df`\n",
        "- Changes to it will not change `df`.\n",
        "- We use `.loc`, the cousin of `.iloc` to update `df`.\n",
        "- **We must also very carefully put all indices, comma separated, in single brackets.**\n",
        "  \t- Row and column, together, within `[]`\n",
        "\n",
        "## Recall: [NumPy](05_numpy.qmd#coordinate-slices)\n",
        "\n",
        ":::{.hidden}\n"
      ],
      "id": "7cb5e38b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "arr = np.array([\n",
        "    [9275, .10],\n",
        "    [37650, .15],\n",
        "    [91150, .25],\n",
        "    [190150, .28],\n",
        "    [413350, .33],\n",
        "    [415051, .35]\n",
        "])"
      ],
      "id": "578ecf2c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "- I recommend using the comma notation.\n",
        "- Otherwise I get unexpected behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: {.columns}\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n"
      ],
      "id": "0f9aa19c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# reverse in both dimensions\n",
        "arr[::-1][::-1] "
      ],
      "id": "4d2ef287",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n"
      ],
      "id": "9d59287c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# reverse in both dimensions\n",
        "arr[::-1,::-1]"
      ],
      "id": "d5f664f2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::::\n",
        "\n",
        "- Takeaway:  Always use `[x,y]` instead of `[x][y]`\n",
        "- This is why we use <s>NumPy</s>**pandas**!\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Update\n"
      ],
      "id": "e1ceade7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.loc[df[\"Metalloid\"] == \"yes\", \"Metallic\"] = \"Metalloid\"\n",
        "df.iloc[12:16][[\"Metal\",\"Nonmetal\",\"Metalloid\",\"Metallic\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "82f12562",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- Much nicer.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Another drop\n",
        "\n",
        "- Let's just keep \"Metallic\" now that we have it."
      ],
      "id": "c87b6965"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Have to specify columns (we can also drop rows)\n",
        "df = df.drop(columns=[\"Metal\",\"Nonmetal\",\"Metalloid\"])\n",
        "df.columns "
      ],
      "id": "a435333a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Groups\n",
        "\n",
        "## On groups\n",
        "\n",
        "- In chemistry:\n",
        "\n",
        "> a group (also known as a family) is a column of elements in the periodic table of the chemical elements.\n",
        "\n",
        "- In computing\n",
        "\n",
        "> a set together with a binary operation satisfying certain algebraic conditions.\n",
        "\n",
        "## On groupby\n",
        "\n",
        "- In **pandas**\n",
        "\n",
        "> A `groupby` operation involves some combination of splitting the object, applying a function, and combining the results. This can be used to group large amounts of data and compute operations on these groups.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Element Groups\n"
      ],
      "id": "5b5b3586"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.iloc[:60:10][[\"Element\",\"Group\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "39d7e69f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Counting\n",
        "\n",
        "- Let's see how many elements are in each group!"
      ],
      "id": "c474a1da"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.groupby(\"Group\").count()"
      ],
      "id": "e72795f4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Goofy\n",
        "\n",
        "- Oh it took the count of all columns.\n",
        "- But... that isn't useless?\n",
        "- Let's perhaps look a melting point by group."
      ],
      "id": "62c98089"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.groupby(\"Group\")[\"MeltingPoint\"].mean()"
      ],
      "id": "6c2172c1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Plot it\n",
        "\n",
        "- That was too hard to see."
      ],
      "id": "24dca0c4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.groupby(\"Group\")[\"MeltingPoint\"].mean().plot()"
      ],
      "id": "7659e3c0",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## `groupby`\n",
        "\n",
        "- Groupby is a cool and powerful tool.\n",
        "- I tend to use it with summary statistics, and you can usually work around it with filters, but with a bit of practice it's a real joy to work with.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Merge \n",
        "\n",
        "## Names\n",
        "\n",
        "- An astute learning will note a few oddities with names and symbols in the table.\n",
        "- Some symbols and elements start with different letters.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Strings\n",
        "\n",
        "- Like lists, arrays, and DataFrames, we can use indices on strings of characters."
      ],
      "id": "27eac212"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "ele = df.iloc[1]\n",
        "symbol = ele[\"Symbol\"]\n",
        "element = ele[\"Element\"]\n",
        "symbol[0] == element[0], symbol, element"
      ],
      "id": "91eaa3f8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Differing\n",
        "\n",
        "- Sometimes the first letter differs - but not often."
      ],
      "id": "55d74611"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "ele = df.iloc[18]\n",
        "symbol = ele[\"Symbol\"]\n",
        "element = ele[\"Element\"]\n",
        "symbol[0] == element[0], symbol, element"
      ],
      "id": "00ba681a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Write a function\n",
        "\n",
        "- We make a function that checks if two things start with the same letter."
      ],
      "id": "ea11e2d5"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def differ(x,y):\n",
        "\t# != means \"does not equal\"\n",
        "\treturn x[0] != y[0]"
      ],
      "id": "f49bcedb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Vectorize\n",
        "\n",
        "- To apply to DataFrames, we must vectorize it.\n",
        "  \t- `x[0]` of a DataFrame is a row\n",
        "\t- `x[0]` of a string is a letter\n",
        "\t- We wanted letters!"
      ],
      "id": "18cbc7b9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "diff_vector = np.vectorize(differ)"
      ],
      "id": "50a7b6d6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## The Elements\n"
      ],
      "id": "68573f36"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df[diff_vector(df[\"Symbol\"], df[\"Element\"])][\"Element\"]"
      ],
      "id": "998152d8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- Basically, these elements have archaic Latin names that were in use when symbols were set before developing their modern names, or something. [Read more](https://www.compoundchem.com/2016/02/02/confusing-elements/)\n",
        "\n",
        "##\n",
        "\n",
        "![](https://i0.wp.com/www.compoundchem.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/02/11-Confusing-Chemical-Element-Symbols-Explained.png)\n",
        "\n",
        "## Adding Names\n",
        "\n",
        "- Let's make another DataFrame\n",
        "  \t- We'll call if `ad` for \"artus datus\", which might (I have no idea) be Latin for data frame."
      ],
      "id": "ba2ea5a8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "names = [\n",
        "    [\"Sodium\", \"Natrium\"],\n",
        "    [\"Potassium\", \"Kalium\"],\n",
        "    [\"Iron\", \"Ferrum\"],\n",
        "    [\"Silver\", \"Argentum\"],\n",
        "    [\"Tin\", \"Stannum\"],\n",
        "    [\"Antimony\", \"Stibium\"],\n",
        "    [\"Tungsten\", \"Wolfram\"],\n",
        "    [\"Gold\", \"Aurum\"],\n",
        "    [\"Mercury\", \"Hydrargyrum\"],\n",
        "    [\"Lead\", \"Plumbum\"]\n",
        "]"
      ],
      "id": "a2778267",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## To DataFrame\n",
        "\n",
        "- `ad` for \"artus datus\", which may be Latin for data frame."
      ],
      "id": "69ef0607"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "ad = pd.DataFrame(names)\n",
        "ad"
      ],
      "id": "a7dee8bb",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Column Names\n",
        "\n",
        "- Remember `.columns`?"
      ],
      "id": "90853d10"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.columns"
      ],
      "id": "fc1f44d5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "- We can actually use `=` with `.columns`"
      ],
      "id": "3ac7cbb2"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "ad.columns = [\"Element\", \"Latin\"] \n",
        "ad.iloc[0]"
      ],
      "id": "88bcb7d3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Merge\n"
      ],
      "id": "18cda4b7"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "df.merge(ad) "
      ],
      "id": "8a765bfd",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Oh no!\n",
        "\n",
        "- So we successfully added Latin names, but...\n",
        "- We lost all elements *without* a Latin name.\n",
        "- The merge only kept elements in both `df` and `ad`\n",
        "\n",
        "# Join\n",
        "\n",
        "## Don't merge\n",
        "\n",
        "- In my experience, merges never work, but...\n",
        "- They explain *how* joins, the good thing, work.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Background\n",
        "\n",
        "- To make learning joins easier, we'll work with two DataFrames."
      ],
      "id": "35a83e9d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "metalloids = df[df[\"Metallic\"] == \"Metalloid\"]\n",
        "latin_name = df.merge(ad)"
      ],
      "id": "dbbdd836",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Index\n",
        "\n",
        "- Versus `merge`, which just figures it out, `join` likes to look at indices.\n",
        "- So we have to make sure we have the same index on both data frames.\n",
        "- We'll use `\"Symbol\"`"
      ],
      "id": "94bdc89d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "metalloids.index = metalloids[\"Symbol\"]\n",
        "latin_name.index = latin_name[\"Symbol\"]"
      ],
      "id": "176ad24c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Simplify\n",
        "\n",
        "- To make things easier on us, let's just only look at a few columns.\n",
        "- We will avoid having two columns of the same name.\n",
        "  \t- This is manageable, but let's learn joins first."
      ],
      "id": "7898ef6f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "metalloids = metalloids[[\"AtomicNumber\",\"Metallic\"]]\n",
        "latin_name = latin_name[[\"Element\",\"Latin\"]]"
      ],
      "id": "8f3b33cf",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## The df's\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: {.columns}\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n"
      ],
      "id": "a1bfc524"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "metalloids"
      ],
      "id": "0dee9fa2",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n"
      ],
      "id": "5f7bcb04"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "latin_name"
      ],
      "id": "53ae7f75",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Visuals\n",
        "\n",
        "- I will also visualize this using venn diagrams.\n",
        "- To my knowledge, there is no graceful way to visualize venn diagrams, but `matplotlib-venn` is okay.\n",
        "- I'm spoiler-marking code that shows how I make the venn diagrams.\n",
        "\n",
        "##\n"
      ],
      "id": "55e589ec"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| code-fold: true\n",
        "from matplotlib_venn import venn2, venn2_circles\n",
        "\n",
        "def show_join(how, left, right, mid):\n",
        "    v = venn2((2, 2, 1), ('metalloids', 'latin_name'))\n",
        "    v.get_patch_by_id('100').set_color('darkblue' if left else 'white')\n",
        "    v.get_patch_by_id('010').set_color('darkblue' if right else 'white')\n",
        "    v.get_patch_by_id('110').set_color('darkblue' if mid else 'white')\n",
        "    v.get_patch_by_id('100').set_alpha(1.0)\n",
        "    v.get_patch_by_id('010').set_alpha(1.0)\n",
        "    v.get_patch_by_id('110').set_alpha(1.0)\n",
        "    venn2_circles((2, 2, 1))\n",
        "    for idx, subset in enumerate(v.subset_labels):\n",
        "        v.subset_labels[idx].set_visible(False)\n",
        "        plt.title(\"metalloids.join(latin_name, how=\\\"\" + how + \"\\\")\")\n",
        "    plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "79c401fa",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Quoth [**pandas**](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.join.html){.smaller}\n",
        "\n",
        "how: `{‘left’,‘right’,‘outer’,‘inner’,‘cross’}`\n",
        "\n",
        "- How to handle operation of the two objects.\n",
        "\n",
        "  - `'left'`: use calling frame’s index  \n",
        "  - `'right'`: use other’s index.\n",
        "  - `'outer'`: form union of calling frame’s index with other’s index.\n",
        "  - `'inner'`: form intersection of calling frame’s index with other’s index.\n",
        "  - `'cross'`: creates the cartesian product from both frames.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Join\n",
        "\n",
        "- Join uses all columns, filling with \"NaN\".\n"
      ],
      "id": "ce8670b1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "metalloids.join(latin_name)"
      ],
      "id": "756e2326",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Left\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: {.columns}\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n"
      ],
      "id": "7bfaea5b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "metalloids.join(latin_name, how='left')[\"Element\"]"
      ],
      "id": "f2d4c76d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n"
      ],
      "id": "6a3a3761"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
